<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Bishopric of Pomesania</h1><p class="st-meta">1243 CE – 1527 CE · capital Riesenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Prussian prince-bishopric on the lower Vistula, closely tied to the Teutonic Order, whose bishop embraced the Reformation in the 1520s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The diocese was one of four created in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a> in 1243, covering the land of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pomesanians">Pomesanians</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> received a third of the territory as his own principality, with the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabuty">Riesenburg</a> (Prabuty) as residence and a cathedral at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> (Kwidzyn).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its cathedral chapter belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> from 1285, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were usually loyal to the Order. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> became the centre of the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorothea-of-montau">Dorothea of Montau</a>, a local mystic. After 1466 the bishopric remained in the Order's part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, under Polish overlordship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erhard-von-queis">Erhard von Queis</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and, after the secularisation of the Order's state into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a> in 1525, surrendered his secular rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Duke Albert</a> in 1527. The territory became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a>, and the Protestant bishopric was later abolished.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bishopric-of-pomesania.jpg" alt="Bishopric of Pomesania" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Radnok · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwidzyn_Cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1243 CE – 1527 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riesenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Erhard von Queis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Bishopric of Pomesania, Bistum Pomesanien</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1385&amp;focus=bishopric-of-pomesania" aria-label="Bishopric of Pomesania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5100 332 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bishopric of Pomesania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5420 588L5451 561M5420 588L5451 561"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5451 561h0M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5420 588h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5420 588h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1385&amp;focus=bishopric-of-pomesania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
